@page "/components/dialogs"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Dialogs

<DocsHeader Title="Dialogs, drawers and toasts" Eyebrow="Components">
    <Lead>Modal dialogs in markup or from code, confirmations, side drawers and toast notifications.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    Dialogs and drawers are a native modal <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>: focus moves into it and stays there, Escape
    closes it, the page behind it is inert, and focus returns to the button that opened it. All of this page needs an
    interactive render mode.
</p>

<h2 id="dialog">Dialog</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(DialogInMarkup)">
    <p>
        Bind <code>@@bind-Open</code> to a field. <code>Title</code> is the heading and the dialog's accessible name.
        Put the actions in <code>Footer</code>, the main action last. The close button in the header is there unless
        <code>ShowClose="false"</code>; its name is <code>CloseLabel</code>. <code>OnClose</code> runs however the
        dialog was closed.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="sizes">Sizes</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Sizes)">
    <p><code>Size</code> is <code>Small</code>, <code>Medium</code> (the default) or <code>Large</code>.</p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="confirm">Confirmations</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Confirm)">
    <p>
        <code>IStilDialogService.ConfirmAsync</code> asks a question and returns <code>true</code> when the user confirms.
        Name the action in <code>ConfirmText</code> ("Delete", not "OK"). With <code>ConfirmVariant="Danger"</code>, focus
        starts on Cancel, so pressing Enter by mistake does not delete anything. The service needs
        <code>&lt;StilDialogHost /&gt;</code> once in an interactive layout.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="from-code">A component in a dialog</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(OpenFromCode)">
    <p>
        <code>OpenAsync&lt;TComponent&gt;</code> shows any component in a dialog and passes it parameters. The component
        gets a cascading <code>StilDialogReference</code>; <code>Close(result)</code> closes the dialog and returns the
        result to the code that opened it. Dismissing the dialog returns <code>null</code>. The component in this
        example:
    </p>
</DocExample>
<CodeBlock Code="@ExampleSource.Get(typeof(RenameDialog))" />

<h2 id="drawer">Drawer</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(FilterDrawer)">
    <p>
        <code>StilDrawer</code> is a dialog that slides in from the side, for filters, details or navigation on small
        screens. It has the same parameters as <code>StilDialog</code>, plus <code>Position</code> (<code>Start</code>,
        the default, or <code>End</code>) and <code>Width</code>.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="toasts">Toasts</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(ToastVariants)">
    <p>
        <code>IStilToastService</code> shows short messages in a corner of the screen: <code>Success</code>,
        <code>Info</code>, <code>Warning</code>, <code>Error</code>, or <code>Show</code> with a variant and a
        duration. They close after <code>StilOptions.DefaultToastDuration</code> (5 seconds);
        <code>TimeSpan.Zero</code> keeps a toast until the user closes it. Error and warning toasts are announced at
        once (<code>role="alert"</code>), the others politely (<code>role="status"</code>). Do not put the only copy of
        important information or an action in a toast: it disappears. Add <code>&lt;StilToastHost /&gt;</code> once to
        an interactive layout.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
<p>Add the hosts once, for example at the end of <code>MainLayout.razor</code>:</p>
<CodeBlock Code="@Hosts" />

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilDialog)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilDrawer)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilToastHost)" />

@code {
    private const string Hosts = """
        <StilDialogHost />
        <StilToastHost />
        """;
}
